{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content \\\"D:\\\\Claude\\\\nemesis\\\\audit.md\\\" -Raw\"","aggregated_output":"# Code Audit, Quality & Security\n\n> Compiled 2026-08-05 from public sources. Every fact in the matrix and every\n> winner callout traces to a fetched URL. Tags: **[F]** = fact, **[A]** = analysis,\n> **?** = unverified. Numbered footnotes¹⁾ inline; full source trail in\n> `## Footnotes` and per-repo evidence blocks in `## Evidence appendix` at the\n> end of the doc.\n\n---\n\n## a. TL;DR\n\n1. **This niche is four sub-niches masquerading as one.** Repo health scoring\n   (RepoDoctor, react-doctor), static-analysis / SAST (opengrep), AI-assisted\n   audit (ai-code-audit, Decepticon), and workflow-level review gates\n   (claude-caliper) share a vocabulary — \"find issues,\" \"score a repo\" — but\n   the *mechanisms* are completely different. **[A]**¹⁾\n2. **The single most important story in the niche right now is `opengrep`\n   (a fork of Semgrep v1.100.0, 2,879 stars, OCaml 5.3.0, LGPL-2.1, 40\n   language parsers, v1.26.0 released 2026-07-24, backed by a 5-vendor\n   AppSec consortium).** It is the politically and technically significant\n   move of the year: Semgrep moved advanced features behind a commercial\n   license; opengrep re-opened them. Existing Semgrep rules work unchanged. **[F]**²⁾\n3. **Deterministic scanners and AI-assisted auditors are not the same\n   thing.** react-doctor (14,279 stars, TypeScript monorepo, oxlint + ESLint\n   plugins, 0-100 health score) and opengrep are rule-based and produce\n   stable, reproducible results. RepoDoctor, ai-code-audit, Decepticon, and\n   claude-caliper are LLM-orchestrated and inherit model variance. **[F]**³⁾\n4. **The single most impressive self-reported benchmark in the roster is\n   Decepticon's 98.08% (102/104) on XBOW validation-benchmarks** — with\n   public LangSmith traces per challenge, beating Shannon Lite (96.15%),\n   Strix (96.15%), PentestGPT (86.5%), the commercial XBOW agent (~85%),\n   Cyber-AutoAgent (84.62%), and MAPTA (76.9%). Decepticon is **not** a code\n   auditor — it is an *attack* agent that runs opengrep-style source review\n   on a *target* system. **[F]**⁴⁾ (`docs/benchmark-comparison.md`)\n5. **Surprising finding: `claude-caliper` is not what the task brief\n   said.** The brief describes it as \"a benchmark harness for the model\n   itself.\" The README explicitly says it is \"a Claude Code plugin that\n   turns your goal into a PR with as little friction as possible.\" There\n   is no model benchmarking; the \"caliper\" is a carpentry metaphor (measure\n   twice, cut once) for sizing work into Small/Medium/Large tiers. It is\n   a *workflow* tool, not a model-evaluation tool. **[A]**⁵⁾\n\n**Top 3 winners overall [A]:**⁶⁾\n1. **opengrep** — best general-purpose SAST (rule-compatible with Semgrep,\n   40 languages, open governance, OCaml 5.3.0 multicore, Cosign-signed\n   binaries, the most politically significant open-source move of the year).\n2. **react-doctor** — best off-the-shelf React-specific audit (14.2k stars,\n   full monorepo with 11 packages — CLI, oxlint plugin, ESLint plugin,\n   language server, GitHub Action, VS Code + Zed extensions, fuzz + evals\n   packages, \"changed scope\" PR mode that only reports PR-introduced issues).\n3. **Decepticon** — best red-team / attack-side tool (XBOW 98.08%, Apache-2.0,\n   16 specialist agents, two-network Docker isolation, tier-mapped model\n   providers, optional cloud product).\n\n---\n\n## b. Scope and methodology\n\n**Roster covered (7 repos, as assigned):**⁷⁾\n\n| # | Repo | Sub-category | Primary URL |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | k1lgor/RepoDoctor | Repo quality (LLM-orchestrated) | https://github.com/k1lgor/RepoDoctor |\n| 2 | millionco/react-doctor | Repo quality (React-specific, deterministic) | https://github.com/millionco/react-doctor |\n| 3 | opengrep/opengrep | Static analysis / SAST (deterministic) | https://github.com/opengrep/opengrep |\n| 4 | kurkowski93/ai-code-audit | AI-assisted audit (multi-perspective) | https://github.com/kurkowski93/ai-code-audit |\n| 5 | PurpleAILAB/Decepticon | AI red-team / pentest agent | https://github.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon |\n| 6 | nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper | Workflow plugin (multi-agent review gates) | https://github.com/nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper |\n| 7 | kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review | Meta-list (AI code review) | https://github.com/kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review |\n\n**What I did for each repo this pass [A]:**⁸⁾\n- Fetched the GitHub repo header via `api.github.com/repos/<owner>/<repo>`\n  (stargazers, language, license, size, default branch, topics, homepage,\n  last push, open issue count).\n- Fetched the canonical `raw.githubusercontent.com/<owner>/<repo>/main/README.md`.\n  For react-doctor, this was `packages/react-doctor/README.md` (the npm package\n  README).\n- For 6 of 7 repos, walked the top-level tree via\n  `api.github.com/repos/<...>/contents/` to surface evidence (architecture\n  diagrams, language server packages, plugin packages, agent files, perspective\n  nodes).\n- For Decepticon, fetched the benchmark-comparison page and the per-challenge\n  benchmark README to surface a verifiable cross-agent leaderboard with\n  LangSmith traces.\n- For claude-caliper, walked the `skills/` directory and read three SKILL.md\n  bodies (design, codebase-review, test-audit) to characterize what it\n  actually enforces.\n- For opengrep, fetched the post-fork changelog `OPENGREP.md` and the latest\n  release via the GitHub API to characterize changes since the v1.100.0 fork.\n- For react-doctor, read the `action.yml` (670 lines), root `package.json`,\n  `packages/core/src/*` and the `AGENTS.md` (~27 KB of dev rules) to\n  characterize the diagnostic engine, scoring system, and CI integration.\n- For awesome-ai-code-review, walked the README and listed the 22 most-cited\n  tools in a \"what's in the list\" sub-section.\n\n**What I could not verify (transparent gaps) [A]:**⁹⁾\n- **claude-caliper is mis-described in the task brief.** The brief calls it\n  \"a measurement of Claude's capabilities (benchmark harness for the model\n  itself).\" The README explicitly contradicts this — it is \"a Claude Code\n  plugin that turns your goal into a PR with as little friction as\n  possible.\" Treated here as a workflow-harness tool, with the discrepancy\n  flagged in the matrix.\n- RepoDoctor has **zero stargazers** (last push 2026-08-03, repo size 266 KB);\n  the count is likely being hidden by the owner or the repo was recently\n  re-initialized. **[?]**¹⁰⁾\n- ai-code-audit is **effectively dormant** (2 stars, 18 KB, last commit\n  2025-03-12 — over 16 months stale). The graph and code structure are\n  sound but the project has no community use; treated as a reference\n  architecture for \"what LLM-driven audit looks like in LangGraph,\" not\n  as a recommended tool.¹¹⁾\n- Decepticon's 98.08% XBOW score is **self-reported** (Decepticon's own\n  benchmark-comparison.md), with public LangSmith traces per challenge.\n  Strix's 96.15% uses a slightly different hint level (description only,\n  no vulnerability tags), so the comparison is **not strictly apples-to-apples**.\n  Both are black-box; Shannon Lite (96.15%) is white-box. **[A]**¹²⁾\n- The Decepticon repo license was confirmed Apache-2.0; the awesome list\n  confirmed CC0; ai-code-audit's README claims MIT but the GitHub API\n  shows `license: null` (no `LICENSE` file at repo root). **[A]**¹³⁾\n\n---\n\n## c. The four sub-categories\n\nThis document covers four distinct sub-categories that share a vocabulary\n(\"find issues,\" \"score a repo\") but have very different mechanisms.¹⁴⁾\n\n### 1. Repo quality (RepoDoctor, react-doctor)\n**What's similar:** Both produce a 0-100 health score and identify anti-patterns\nacross multiple dimensions. Both are marketed as \"what's wrong with my\ncodebase\" tools, and both can be wired into a developer's local workflow.¹⁵⁾\n**What's different:** RepoDoctor (Python, ~0 stars, v0.2.0) is LLM-orchestrated —\nit ships a prompt library and delegates analysis to GitHub Copilot CLI. react-doctor\n(TypeScript, 14,279 stars, v0.9.5) is deterministic — it runs AST-based rules\nand counts issues. RepoDoctor is a \"Copilot-first architecture\" with prompts\nin `src/repodoc/prompts/v1/`.¹⁶⁾ react-doctor is a full monorepo with 11 packages\nincluding oxlint + ESLint plugins, a language server, two editor extensions,\nand a fuzz testing harness.¹⁷⁾ RepoDoctor's \"TOUR.md\" feature is unique — neither\nopengrep, nor ai-code-audit, nor react-doctor produces an onboarding tour.\nreact-doctor's \"changed scope\" PR mode (only report issues the PR introduced,\nnot existing backlog) is the killer feature for CI gate use.¹⁸⁾ **[A]**\n\n### 2. Static analysis / security (opengrep)\n**What's similar:** opengrep is the only rule-based SAST tool in the roster.\nThe closest cousins are ESLint/oxlint (used by react-doctor) and the \"Security\n& Static Analysis AI\" section of the awesome-ai-code-review list (Snyk\nDeepCode, Semgrep AI). opengrep is the *lineage* — Semgrep v1.100.0 was\nforked in 2024-12-14 into opengrep after Semgrep moved advanced features\nbehind a commercial license.¹⁹⁾\n**What's different:** opengrep is the only tool in the roster that produces\nSARIF output (`--sarif-output=sarif.json`) and supports 40 languages via\n40 parser directories.²⁰⁾ It is the only tool with a Trusted Code Base (`TCB/`)\nof capability-based IO sandboxes in OCaml (`Cap.ml`, `CapUnix.ml`, `CapSys.ml`).²¹⁾\nIt is the only tool with consortium funding (Aikido, Amplify, Endor Labs,\nKodem, Orca Security) and a published manifesto (opengrep.dev).²²⁾ It is also\nthe only tool that ships Cosign-signed releases.²³⁾ opengrep is the **only**\nnon-LLM static analyzer in the roster. **[A]**\n\n### 3. AI-assisted audit (ai-code-audit, Decepticon)\n**What's similar:** Both use LLMs as the analysis engine. Both run as\nmulti-agent systems. Both produce Markdown / Neo4j-persisted reports.²⁴⁾\n**What's different:** ai-code-audit (2 stars, 18 KB, last commit\n2025-03-12) is a defensive source-code auditor — it reads a GitHub repo\nand produces per-perspective reports. Decepticon (4,984 stars, 309 MB,\nApache-2.0, 16 specialist agents) is an offensive attack agent — it\nruns *against* a target system, exploits vulnerabilities, and chains\ntechniques. They are at opposite ends of the offensive-vs-defensive\nspectrum. Decepticon's XBOW score (98.08%) is for *successful exploitation*;\nai-code-audit's \"score\" is a list of findings with no published benchmark.\nai-code-audit is dormant; Decepticon is actively developed (v1.1.40\nreleased 2026-07-27).²⁵⁾ **[A]**\n\n### 4. Model evaluation (claude-caliper) + meta-listing (awesome-ai-code-review)\n**What's similar:** Neither is a code auditor in the traditional sense.\nclaude-caliper is a Claude Code *plugin* (16 skills, fresh-context review\nsubagents, 0-100 design-doc gate) that orchestrates code production with\nquality gates.²⁶⁾ awesome-ai-code-review is a curated list of 50+ AI code\nreview tools and 14+ research papers.²⁷⁾\n**What's different:** claude-caliper is a workflow tool that *uses* Claude\nCode; the README is explicit that the \"caliper\" is a carpentry metaphor\n(measure twice, cut once) for sizing work, not a model benchmark. The\nawesome list is passive — it catalogs tools maintained by Kodus, with\n19 contributors and a disclosed maintainer bias.²⁸⁾ The \"model evaluation\"\nframing in the task brief is misleading; claude-caliper's `skill-eval`\nskill is the only place it evaluates anything, and it grades *other\nskills*, not the model itself. **[A]**\n\n---\n\n## d. Full feature matrix (16 columns × 7 rows)\n\n| # | Tool | Purpose (1 line) | Primary language / stack | Mechanism | Input (data consumed) | Output (what it produces) | LLM/agent integration | Distribution / install | License | Cost model | Local-first? | Maturity (stars, last commit, version, age) | Performance / coverage (with sources) | False-positive rate (if known) | Notable differentiator | Failure mode (1 line) | Stack position |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **RepoDoctor** (k1lgor) | AI-powered repo health analysis that delegates to GitHub Copilot CLI. [F]²⁹⁾ | Python 3.11+, typer, pydantic, rich. [F] | Prompt templates → subprocess to Copilot CLI → JSON → Pydantic validation → terminal / JSON / Markdown. [F] | Local repo (file tree + selected files). [F] | Multi-module scores, aggregate health score, Markdown report. [F] | **Yes** — by design. Uses Copilot CLI as the engine. [F] | `pip install repodoc` or `uv tool install repodoc`. [F] | MIT [F] | Free; requires Copilot subscription. [F] | Yes (except Copilot's outbound). [A] | 0 stars (hidden), v0.2.0, last commit 2026-08-03, age ~6 mo, 266 KB. [F] | 6 dimensions: diet, tour, docker, deadcode, scan, report. No published benchmark. [F] | Not published. [A] | \"Copilot-first\" — prompts are first-class artifacts. [F] | Without authenticated Copilot, cannot function. [F] | Local CLI audit. [A] |\n| 2 | **react-doctor** (millionco) | Deterministic React linter (state/effects, perf, arch, security, a11y). [F]³⁰⁾ | TypeScript monorepo (pnpm + turbo), oxc-parser, Effect. [F] | AST scans of `.tsx`/`.jsx` → 5 categories → JSON diagnostics → 0-100 health score. No LLM. [F] | Local repo (.tsx, .jsx, framework entries); optional `--diff` vs base. [F] | JSON report, terminal output, inline PR review comments, sticky PR comment, 0-100 score. [F] | **No** LLM in scanner. Teaches agents via `npx react-doctor@latest install`. [F] | `npx react-doctor@latest`, npm. [F] | MIT [F] | Free; opt-out telemetry to Sentry. [F] | Yes. [F] | 14,279 stars, 455 forks, 62 issues, last commit 2026-08-05, v0.9.5, age ~6 mo, 115 MB. [F] | 5 categories on Next/Vite/Astro/TanStack/React Native/Expo. Fuzz + evals packages. [F] | Not published as one number. [A] | **\"changed\" scope** on PRs (only PR-introduced issues, not backlog). [F] | Deterministic → recall ceiling; de-slop subpackage covers AI-coding artifacts only. [A] | Pre-commit / CI gate. [F] |\n| 3 | **opengrep** (opengrep) | OSS SAST forked from Semgrep v1.100.0, 30+ languages, rule-compatible. [F]³¹⁾ | OCaml 5.3.0 (shared-memory multicore). Self-contained binaries via Nuitka. [F] | AST pattern matching with custom YAML DSL. Taint analysis (`--taint-intrafile`) with higher-order functions. [F] | Source files + rule YAML + optional semgrepignore. [F] | Terminal table; JSON; SARIF 2.1.0 (metavariables, fingerprints, enclosing context). [F] | **No** LLM in engine. Invokable from pre-commit, CI, or directly. [A] | `curl -fsSL .../install.sh \\| bash` (~30-44 MB binaries, Cosign-signed). [F] | LGPL-2.1 [F] | Free; consortium-backed. [F] | Yes. [F] | 2,879 stars, 235 forks, 84 issues, last commit 2026-08-05, v1.26.0 (2026-07-24), 91 MB, age ~20 mo. [F] | 40 language parsers; OWASP, CWE Top 25, all Semgrep rules work. [F] | Not published; AST matching → \"very high precision.\" [A] | **The fork itself** — keeps advanced features open. [F] | Same as Semgrep: pattern-based → recall ceiling for inter-procedural. [A] | Pre-commit hook + CI gate. [F] |\n| 4 | **ai-code-audit** (kurkowski93) | LangGraph agent auditing repos from 5 perspectives (arch, business, quality, security, modernization). [F]³²⁾ | Python. LangGraph, LangChain, OpenAI SDK, PyGithub, GitPython, Tavily. [F] | StateGraph: `starting → repository_scanner → 5 perspectives parallel → generate_report`. [F] | GitHub repo URL + OpenAI key + GITHUB_TOKEN + TAVILY_API_KEY. [F] | Per-perspective reports + combined report + Q&A. [F] | **Yes** — five parallel LLM agents. OpenAI-only. [F] | `pip install -r requirements.txt; langgraph run agent`. [F] | MIT (claimed; SPDX missing). [A] | Free; user pays OpenAI. [A] | Yes (code only). [A] | 2 stars, 0 forks, last commit 2025-03-12 (stale ~16 mo), 18 KB. [F] | 5 perspectives, no published benchmark. [A] | Not published. [A] | Five-perspective parallel graph decomposition. [A] | Dormant; OpenAI-only; no version; no CI gate. [A] | Local agent, on demand. [A] |\n| 5 | **Decepticon** (PurpleAILAB) | Autonomous AI Red Team agent — executes full kill chains (recon → exploit → lateral). [F]³³⁾ | Python (LangGraph), LiteLLM, Docker Compose, PostgreSQL, Neo4j, tmux. [F] | 16 specialist agents, fresh context per objective, two-network Docker (mgmt vs sandbox). [F] | Engagement brief + target system (Kali sandbox, optional C2, victim targets). [F] | Neo4j-persisted findings, terminal CLI, optional web dashboard. [F] | **Yes** — by design. Tier-mapped providers (Anthropic, OpenAI, Gemini, MiniMax, DeepSeek, xAI, Mistral, OpenRouter, NIM, Ollama). [F] | `curl -fsSL https://decepticon.red/install \\| bash`; or `pip install decepticon`. [F] | Apache-2.0 [F] | Free; optional cloud product at app.decepticon.red; user pays LLM. [F] | Yes (Docker); cloud option exists. [F] | 4,984 stars, 971 forks, 1 issue, last commit 2026-08-05, v1.1.40 (2026-07-27), 309 MB, age ~14 mo. [F] | **XBOW 98.08% (102/104)**: L1 100% (45/45), L2 98.0% (50/51), L3 87.5% (7/8). 23 attack classes. [F] | N/A (pass rate metric). [A] | **Offensive Vaccine loop** (planned) + two-network isolation so compromised sandbox can't reach LLM gateway. [F] | Requires reachable target; RoE/ConOps discipline is user's responsibility; 2/104 unsolved. [F] | **Red-team agent** — not a code auditor. [A] |\n| 6 | **claude-caliper** (nikhilsitaram) | Claude Code plugin (16 skills) that turns a feature description into a PR with design-gated TDD + fresh-context review. [F]³⁴⁾ | Shell-driven Claude Code plugin. 16 skills, plan.json (schema 2). [F] | Pipeline: design → design-review → implement (TDD) → impl-review → pr-create → pr-review → pr-merge. Each review = fresh subagent. [F] | A user feature description. [F] | A pull request (squash-merged) + design doc + plan.json + reviews.json. [F] | **Yes** — uses Claude Code as substrate, spawns fresh subagents. [F] | `/plugin marketplace add nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper; /plugin install claude-caliper@claude-caliper`. [F] | MIT [F] | Free; uses user's Claude subscription. [A] | Yes. [A] | 88 stars, 7 forks, 3 issues, last commit 2026-07-15, v2.0.0, age ~5 mo, 1.4 MB. [F] | 9-point design checklist, TDD enforcement, fresh-context review. `codebase-review`, `test-audit` (A-F grade), `skill-eval`. [F] | Not a static analyzer. [A] | **Ceremony proportional to size** (v2 tagline). Small/Medium = inline TDD; Large = plan + parallel worktree subagents. [F] | Requires Claude Code (not API). macOS-only for queue/usage-guard. 5-hour usage window. [F] | **Code workflow layer** (not a review agent per se). [A] |\n| 7 | **awesome-ai-code-review** (kodustech) | Curated Markdown list of AI code review tools + research papers + benchmarks. [F]³⁵⁾ | None (Markdown). [F] | 7 sections: PR Agents, IDE Copilots, Research Papers, Security/SAST AI, CLI/Local, Open-Source Models, Benchmarks. [F] | N/A. [A] | Categorized list of links. [A] | Indirect — catalogs tools that integrate LLMs. [A] | Read the Markdown or https://aicodereviews.io. [F] | CC0 [F] | Free. [F] | Yes. [A] | 39 stars, 33 forks, 10 issues, last commit 2026-06-28, age ~8 mo, 38 KB, 19 contributors. [F] | ~50 tool entries, 14+ papers, 4+ benchmarks. [F] | N/A. [A] | Scoped to **code REVIEW** (vs. `awesome-ai-coding-tools`). [F] | Bus factor ~1 (top contributor has 27/30 commits); maintainer bias disclosed (Kodus). [F] | **Discovery layer.** [A] |\n\n---\n\n## e. Best-in-class per dimension\n\nFor each of the 16 columns, a single winner with sourced justification.³⁶⁾\n\n| # | Dimension | Winner | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Purpose (clarity)** | **Decepticon** | Most clearly-scoped README — three paragraphs from tagline to mechanism, with the architecture diagram up-front. The \"AI + hacking\" critique (\"Another AI hacker? Let us guess — it runs nmap and writes a report\") is the most honest positioning in the roster. [F] README L46-60 |\n| 2 | **Primary language / stack** | **opengrep** | OCaml 5.3.0 with shared-memory multicore is the most distinctive engineering choice in the roster. Self-contained Nuitka binaries (30-44 MB) sidestep the Python distribution problem. Cosign-signed. [F] OPENGREP.md |\n| 3 | **Mechanism (technical depth)** | **opengrep** | The combination of AST pattern matching + YAML DSL + taint analysis (`--taint-intrafile`) with higher-order function support is the most sophisticated mechanism in the doc. Plus a Trusted Code Base (`TCB/`) of capability-based IO sandboxes. [F] |\n| 4 | **Input (data consumed)** | **Decepticon** | Most expressive input — a full engagement brief (RoE, ConOps, Deconfliction Plan, OPPLAN with MITRE ATT&CK) + a target system. The discipline layer (RoE first) is unique. [F] README L120-130 |\n| 5 | **Output (what it produces)** | **opengrep** | SARIF 2.1.0 output (the industry standard for SAST) with metavariables, fingerprints, and enclosing context. JSON output with `--inline-metavariables`. The output schema is the most rigorously specified in the doc. [F] README \"To obtain SARIF output\" + OPENGREP.md |\n| 6 | **LLM/agent integration** | **Decepticon** | 16 specialist agents with fresh-context windows, tier-mapped providers (HIGH/MID/LOW per agent), 10 LLM providers + 6 OAuth subscription handlers. The broadest integration surface. [F] README \"Models & Providers\" + docs/models.md |\n| 7 | **Distribution / install** | **react-doctor** | `npx react-doctor@latest` is a one-liner that runs immediately. Plus the `install` (agent skill) and `ci install` (GitHub Action) variants. The most frictionless onboarding in the doc. [F] packages/react-doctor/README.md |\n| 8 | **License** | **Decepticon** | Apache-2.0 is the most permissive of the strong-copyleft/MIT licenses in the doc. Allows commercial use without the LGPL's linking/source-distribution concerns. [F] |\n| 9 | **Cost model** | **opengrep** + **react-doctor** | Both 100% free, OSS, no SaaS-only requirement, no per-user billing. opengrep's consortium backing is a different kind of \"free\" — paid for by 5 AppSec vendors, not VC. [F] |\n| 10 | **Local-first?** | **opengrep** | Self-contained binaries, no SaaS, no telemetry. The only tool in the doc with Cosign-signed releases. [F] |\n| 11 | **Maturity** | **react-doctor** | 14,279 stars, 455 forks, 62 open issues, 5 npm packages released 2026-08-05, AGENTS.md is 27 KB of dev rules. Most actively-developed repo in the doc. [F] |\n| 12 | **Performance / coverage** | **opengrep** | 40 language parsers + all Semgrep rulesets (OWASP, CWE Top 25, etc.) work unchanged. The broadest coverage in the doc. [F] |\n| 13 | **False-positive rate** | **opengrep** | AST matching is deterministic and the property of \"very high precision\" is well-established for Semgrep. Decepticon's metric is \"pass rate,\" not FP. LLM tools in the doc don't publish FP. [A] |\n| 14 | **Notable differentiator** | **opengrep** | The fork itself — keeping advanced SAST features open after Semgrep moved them behind a commercial license. The most politically and technically significant open-source move of the year. [F] |\n| 15 | **Failure mode (most honest)** | **Decepticon** | The disclaimer (\"Do not use this project on any system or network without explicit written authorization\") is the most legally rigorous in the doc. The README's \"What is Decepticon?\" is also the most explicit about what the tool is NOT (\"Another AI hacker? Let us guess — it runs nmap and writes a report. That's not what this is.\"). [F] |\n| 16 | **Stack position** | **react-doctor** | The only tool in the doc with a complete pre-commit + CI gate + editor integration + language server + lint plugin story. The closest to \"drop-in for any team.\" [F] |\n\n**Tally from the table above:** opengrep wins 7 dimensions (2, 3, 5, 10, 12, 13, 14); Decepticon wins 5 dimensions (1, 4, 6, 8, 15); react-doctor wins 3 dimensions outright (7, 11, 16) and shares dimension 9 (Cost model) with opengrep as a tie.³⁷⁾ RepoDoctor, ai-code-audit, claude-caliper, and awesome-ai-code-review each win 0. Three-way split at the top — opengrep wins the \"different story\" (the fork), Decepticon wins the \"moat\" (the benchmark), react-doctor wins the \"drop-in\" (the integration breadth). **[A]**\n\n---\n\n## f. Category winners\n\nFor each of the 7 sub-categories, the best tool per use case.³⁸⁾\n\n### Best for repo health scoring → **RepoDoctor** (or react-doctor for React)\nRepoDoctor's \"Copilot-first architecture\" is the most flexible — adding a\nnew check is a new prompt template, not a new AST rule.³⁹⁾ BUT RepoDoctor\nrequires GitHub Copilot authentication, so for a zero-dependency score,\nreact-doctor's 0-100 health score (with 14.2k stars of community validation)\nis the safer pick. **[A]**\n\n### Best for React-specific audit → **react-doctor**\nNo contest. 14,279 stars, 11 packages, fuzz + evals, the \"changed scope\"\nPR mode, blocking PR mode, health score exposed as a CI output, an oxlint\nplugin for editor-time feedback, and a language server for IDE\nintegration.⁴⁰⁾ RepoDoctor is general-purpose; react-doctor is the\nReact-specific specialist. **[A]**\n\n### Best for static analysis → **opengrep**\nThe fork from Semgrep v1.100.0 keeps the entire Semgrep rule ecosystem\nworking (OWASP, CWE Top 25, plus thousands of community rules) while\nmoving the advanced features (intra-file taint analysis, Apex/Elixir/\nCrystal/Visual Basic, OCaml 5.3.0 multicore, Cosign-signed binaries)\ninto the open.⁴¹⁾ Backed by a 5-vendor AppSec consortium. The most\npolitically and technically significant SAST move of 2025-2026. **[F]**\n\n### Best for AI-assisted code review → **ai-code-audit** (or claude-caliper's `pr-review`)\nai-code-audit's five-perspective parallel graph is the right *shape* for\nan AI-assisted audit (decompose by viewpoint, aggregate).⁴²⁾ But it is\ndormant (16 months stale). For an actively-maintained alternative,\nclaude-caliper's `pr-review` skill does fresh-context review before\nexternal feedback. **[A]**\n\n### Best for LLM security / red-team → **Decepticon**\nXBOW 98.08% with public LangSmith traces.⁴³⁾ Apache-2.0, 16 specialist\nagents, two-network isolation, tier-mapped model providers. No other\ntool in the roster (or in the broader ecosystem, per the benchmark\nleaderboard) is in the same ballpark. **[F]**\n\n### Best for measuring Claude itself → **claude-caliper** (with caveat)\n**Caveat: claude-caliper does not actually measure Claude as a model.**\nThe README is explicit that it is a workflow plugin.⁴⁴⁾ The closest thing\nto \"measuring\" in the plugin is the `skill-eval` skill (assertion-based\ngrading of other skills). For actual Claude model evaluation, the\nawesome-ai-code-review list points to SWE-bench, HumanEval, and\nCode Review Benchmark — none of which are in the roster. **[A]**\n\n### Best for discovering more tools → **awesome-ai-code-review**\n50+ tools, 14+ research papers, 4+ benchmarks across 7 categories.⁴⁵⁾\nMaintained by Kodus. CC0 licensed. The bus factor is low but the\ncontent is high quality. **[F]**\n\n---\n\n## g. Cross-cutting findings⁴⁶⁾\n\n1. **Deterministic vs AI-assisted is the major axis.** The roster splits\n   cleanly: opengrep and react-doctor are deterministic (rules + AST).\n   RepoDoctor, ai-code-audit, Decepticon, and claude-caliper are\n   AI-assisted. The \"best\" pick depends on your tolerance for variance:\n   deterministic = reproducible, AI-assisted = context-aware. **[A]**\n2. **The opengrep/semgrep fork is a politically and technically significant\n   move.** Semgrep Inc. moved advanced features (intra-file taint, certain\n   languages) behind a commercial license. The opengrep consortium\n   (Aikido, Amplify, Endor Labs, Kodem, Orca Security) forked v1.100.0\n   and re-opened them.⁴⁷⁾ This is the first major open-source split in\n   the SAST space, and it sets a precedent for the next 5 years. **[F]**\n3. **Repo health scoring is converging on a 0-100 model.** Both RepoDoctor\n   and react-doctor expose a 0-100 health score.⁴⁸⁾ RepoDoctor's score is\n   LLM-generated and opaque. react-doctor's is computed from error/warning\n   counts and is reproducible. The \"0-100\" convention is also used in\n   code quality tools like Code Climate (out of roster) and is the\n   most common rubric format in the ecosystem. **[A]**\n4. **Model evaluation is its own discipline — and not in this doc.** The\n   brief suggested claude-caliper was a model benchmark. It is not.\n   Model evaluation tools (SWE-bench, HumanEval, lm-evaluation-harness,\n   MMLU) are a separate category. The awesome-ai-code-review list\n   includes SWE-bench and HumanEval. None of the 7 repos in this\n   doc does model evaluation proper. **[A]**\n5. **Meta-lists proliferate — every niche has 2-3.**⁴⁹⁾ The\n   \"AI coding\" and \"AI code review\" niches are close enough that the\n   lists overlap (Cursor, Claude Code, Copilot appear in both). The\n   narrower scope of awesome-ai-code-review (review-specific + papers +\n   benchmarks) is what differentiates it. **[A]**\n6. **Audit-grade tools tend to be CI-gated, not LLM-call-time.** opengrep\n   is a pre-commit hook + CI action. react-doctor is a GitHub Action\n   (action.yml, 670 lines, blocking mode). Decepticon is a full\n   engagement (not a per-PR call). claude-caliper is the *opposite* —\n   it runs at LLM-call-time as a workflow plugin. The exception\n   proves the rule: AI-assisted review lives where the AI is, while\n   deterministic review lives where the code is. **[A]**\n7. **What's MISSING from this niche:**\n   - No **compliance-as-code** tool (SOC2/SLSA/EU AI Act control\n     mapping). heygrc (in the awesome list) is the closest, but it's\n     PR-review-shaped, not a control framework.\n   - No **dependency-vulnerability** tool (Snyk, Dependabot,\n     Trivy). Not in roster.\n   - No **SBOM/SLSA provenance** tool (in-toto, sigstore, Witness).\n     Not in roster.\n   - No **fuzzing** tool (AFL++, libFuzzer, cargo-fuzz). Not in roster.\n   - No **formal verification** tool. Not in roster.\n   - No **test-quality** benchmark in the roster. claude-caliper's\n     `test-audit` is the closest, but it grades the test suite, not\n     the tests against an oracle. **[A]**\n8. **The compliance / regulation story is real but underdeveloped.** The\n   EU AI Act, SOC2, SLSA v1.0, the US Executive Order on AI, and\n   ISO 42001 are all converging on \"audit your AI systems.\" Decepticon\n   audits *targets*; nothing in this doc audits *AI systems themselves*\n   (model cards, training data lineage, eval results). The gap is\n   acknowledged but not filled. **[A]**\n9. **The \"caliper\" metaphor is a workflow metaphor, not a measurement one.**\n   claude-caliper is named after \"measure twice, cut once\" — sizing the\n   work before doing it. It is a *work-sizing* tool, not a *model-benchmarking*\n   tool. The name is misleading to anyone expecting a benchmark. **[A]**\n10. **Decepticon's two-network isolation pattern is the security template\n    for AI agents.** Management on `decepticon-net` (LLM proxy, DBs, API),\n    operations on `sandbox-net` (Kali, C2, targets). The agent reaches\n    the sandbox only via the Docker socket (no TCP from management to\n    operations). Neo4j is the only cross-network service because the\n    sandbox writes findings and the agent reads them.⁵⁰⁾ This pattern\n    (network segmentation + shared minimal data plane) is the right\n    template for any future agent that combines LLM calls with\n    privileged operations. **[F]** architecture.md\n\n---\n\n## i. References\n\nAll URLs accessed on 2026-08-05 unless otherwise noted.\n\n### Repo metadata (GitHub API)\n- https://api.github.com/repos/k1lgor/RepoDoctor\n- https://api.github.com/repos/m