/repos/kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review/contributors\n\n---\n\n## Evidence appendix\n\nPer-repo evidence blocks, in matrix order. Each block lists the source URLs,\nfile paths to the core implementation, exact versions, raw performance numbers,\nand any \"unverified\" markers. The numbered footnotes above are the *short\nform*; the appendix is the *long form* of the same evidence.\n\n### k1lgor/RepoDoctor\n\n- **Source URLs**:\n  - https://github.com/k1lgor/RepoDoctor\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/k1lgor/RepoDoctor/main/README.md\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/k1lgor/RepoDoctor\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/k1lgor/RepoDoctor/main/pyproject.toml\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/k1lgor/RepoDoctor/contents/src/repodoc/commands\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/k1lgor/RepoDoctor/contents/src/repodoc/core\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/k1lgor/RepoDoctor/contents/src/repodoc/prompts\n- **File paths to core implementation**:\n  - `src/repodoc/cli.py` (Typer-based CLI)\n  - `src/repodoc/commands/{base,deadcode,diet,docker,report,scan,tour}.py` (6 subcommands)\n  - `src/repodoc/core/{copilot,exceptions,logger,parser}.py` (Copilot CLI subprocess + JSON parser + Pydantic)\n  - `src/repodoc/prompts/templates.py`, `src/repodoc/prompts/v1/` (prompt library)\n- **Exact versions**: v0.2.0, 2 releases, last commit 2026-08-03, created 2026-02-10 (~6 months old), 266 KB repo, Python classifier \"3 - Alpha\".\n- **Raw performance numbers**:\n  - 6 dimensions: diet (bloat + hygiene), tour (onboarding), docker (Dockerfile security), deadcode (unused code w/ confidence), scan (multi-module), report (Markdown)\n  - \"Automatic retry logic on parse failures\" — README L218\n  - No published benchmark; quality bounded by Copilot's capability per prompt\n- **Unverified**: zero stargazers (likely hidden or recent re-init) **[?]**\n- **Other**: `--in-place` flag will overwrite Dockerfiles — default is \"safe by default (never overwrites without --in-place)\" [README L220]. No GitHub Action shipped. Dead-code detection is \"potentially unused\" with high/medium/low confidence.\n\n### millionco/react-doctor\n\n- **Source URLs**:\n  - https://github.com/millionco/react-doctor\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/millionco/react-doctor/main/packages/react-doctor/README.md\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/millionco/react-doctor\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/millionco/react-doctor/main/action.yml\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/millionco/react-doctor/main/package.json\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/millionco/react-doctor/contents/packages\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/millionco/react-doctor/main/packages/core/package.json\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/millionco/react-doctor/main/AGENTS.md\n  - https://react.doctor (website)\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/millionco/react-doctor/contents/docs\n- **File paths to core implementation**:\n  - 11 packages in the monorepo: `api`, `core`, `deslop-cli`, `deslop-js`, `eslint-plugin-react-doctor`, `evals`, `fuzz`, `language-server`, `oxlint-plugin-react-doctor`, `react-doctor`, `vscode-react-doctor`, `zed-react-doctor`\n  - Core diagnostic pipeline (in `packages/core/src/`): `build-diagnostic-pipeline.ts`, `calculate-score.ts`, `build-json-report.ts`, `classify-file-context.ts`, `compute-diagnostic-delta.ts`, `diagnostic-surface.ts`\n  - AGENTS.md (26.9 KB) codifies dev rules: `@antfu/ni` for installs, truffler for symbol dedup, oxc-parser-based search before adding utilities\n- **Exact versions**: v0.9.5 (5 npm packages released 2026-08-05), 14,279 stars, 455 forks, 62 open issues, last commit 2026-08-05, created 2026-02-13 (~6 months old), 115 MB monorepo.\n- **Raw performance numbers**:\n  - 5 categories: state & effects, performance, architecture, security, accessibility\n  - Works on: Next.js, Vite, Astro, TanStack, React Native, Expo\n  - Has fuzz testing (`@react-doctor/fuzz`) + evals (`@react-doctor/evals`) + `test:public-react-repos` script (scans real public repos for regressions)\n  - GitHub Action (action.yml, 670 lines) exposes 14 inputs and 6 outputs (score, total-issues, fixed-issues, error-count, warning-count, ...)\n  - \"changed\" scope by default — only reports issues the PR introduced vs the base branch\n  - Suppression tooling: `apply-ignore-overrides.ts`, `apply-severity-controls.ts`, `detect-foreign-disable-near-miss.ts`\n  - License: MIT (GitHub API shows NOASSERTION because the LICENSE is at repo root)\n  - Telemetry: Sentry crash + run-trace counters; opt-out via `--no-telemetry`. Sentry endpoint is for anonymous counters only (no file contents)\n- **Unverified**: none material.\n- **Other**: license field on the GitHub API returns `NOASSERTION` because the LICENSE file is at repo root (not in packages).\n\n### opengrep/opengrep\n\n- **Source URLs**:\n  - https://github.com/opengrep/opengrep\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/opengrep/opengrep/main/README.md\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/opengrep/opengrep/main/OPENGREP.md\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/opengrep/opengrep/main/.pre-commit-hooks.yaml\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/opengrep/opengrep\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/opengrep/opengrep/contents/languages\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/opengrep/opengrep/releases/latest\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/opengrep/opengrep/contents/TCB\n  - https://opengrep.dev/\n- **File paths to core implementation**:\n  - 27 subdirectories under `src/`: analyzing, call_graph, configuring, core, core_cli, core_scan, data, engine, experiments, fixing, il, main, matching, metachecking, naming, optimizing, osemgrep, parsing, parsing_languages, printing, reporting, rpc, rule, sca, tainting, target, targeting, tests, typing\n  - `TCB/` (Trusted Code Base) contains `Cap.ml`, `CapUnix.ml`, `CapSys.ml` — capability-based IO sandboxes for the OCaml stdlib\n  - `parsing_languages/` is 40 directories: apex, bash, cairo, circom, cpp, crystal, csharp, dart, dockerfile, elixir, go, hack, html, java, javascript, json, jsonnet, julia, kotlin, lisp, lua, move_on_aptos, move_on_sui, ocaml, php, promql, protobuf, python, ql, r, regexp, ruby, rust, scala, solidity, swift, terraform, typescript, vbnet, yaml\n- **Exact versions**: 2,879 stars, 235 forks, 84 open issues, last commit 2026-08-05, latest release v1.26.0 (2026-07-24), 91 MB repo. Fork created 2024-12-14 (~20 months old).\n- **Raw performance numbers**:\n  - 30+ languages (or 40 by parser count): Apex, Bash, C, C++, C#, Clojure, Crystal, Dart, Dockerfile, Elixir, Go, HTML, Java, JavaScript, JSON, Jsonnet, JSX, Julia, Kotlin, Lisp, Lua, OCaml, PHP, Python, R, Ruby, Rust, Scala, Scheme, Solidity, Swift, Terraform, TSX, TypeScript, Visual Basic, XML, YAML + Generic\n  - 4 platforms × 2 architectures = 8 binaries per release (linux/macOS/windows × aarch64/x86); asset sizes 30-44 MB compressed\n  - Languages **not in Semgrep CE or Pro**: Crystal, Visual Basic (OPENGREP.md L14-16)\n  - Languages **not in Semgrep CE**: Apex, Elixir (OPENGREP.md L17-19)\n  - OWASP, CWE Top 25, plus community rules all work unchanged\n  - Self-contained binaries via Nuitka\n  - Cosign-signed releases\n- **Unverified**: false-positive rate not published as a single number; AST matching inherits Semgrep's \"very high precision\" reputation.\n- **Other**: LGPL-2.1 (preserves Semgrep engine's license for compatibility). Funded by 5-vendor consortium (Aikido, Amplify, Endor Labs, Kodem, Orca Security). Manifesto at opengrep.dev with open governance (\"contributions accepted on merit, not commercial interest\").\n\n### kurkowski93/ai-code-audit\n\n- **Source URLs**:\n  - https://github.com/kurkowski93/ai-code-audit\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kurkowski93/ai-code-audit/main/README.md\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/kurkowski93/ai-code-audit\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kurkowski93/ai-code-audit/main/my_agent/agent.py\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kurkowski93/ai-code-audit/main/langgraph.json\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kurkowski93/ai-code-audit/main/requirements.txt\n- **File paths to core implementation**:\n  - `my_agent/agent.py` — the LangGraph StateGraph definition\n  - `langgraph.json` — exposes the graph as `agent` for `langgraph run`\n  - 5 perspective nodes: `architectural_perspective_node`, `business_domain_perspective_node`, `code_quality_perspective_node`, `security_perspective_node`, `modernization_perspective_node`\n  - StateGraph: `START → starting_node → {repository_scanner | answer_question}`; `repository_scanner → 5 perspective nodes in parallel`; all 5 → `generate_report` → `END`; `answer_question → END`\n- **Exact versions**: 2 stars, 0 forks, 0 open issues, last commit 2025-03-12 (over 16 months stale), created 2025-03-10, 18 KB repo (no version tag).\n- **Raw performance numbers**:\n  - 5 perspective categories, no published benchmark\n  - OpenAI-only (`langchain-openai`)\n  - Default LLM: OpenAI via `langchain-openai`\n  - Required env: `OPENAI_API_KEY`, optional `GITHUB_TOKEN`, `TAVILY_API_KEY`\n  - No PyPI package, no Docker image\n- **Unverified**: License is MIT per README \"License\" section, but GitHub API returns `null` because no `LICENSE` file is in the repo root. README claims MIT; SPDX file is missing. **[A]**\n- **Other**: the only tool in this doc using parallel perspective decomposition (RepoDoctor runs sequentially; react-doctor is rule-based and parallel by category; ai-code-audit decomposes by *perspective*; RepoDoctor decomposes by *artifact type*).\n\n### PurpleAILAB/Decepticon\n\n- **Source URLs**:\n  - https://github.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/main/README.md\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/main/benchmark/results/README.md\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/main/docs/benchmark-comparison.md\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/main/docs/architecture.md\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/main/TELEMETRY.md\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/contents/docs\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/releases\n  - https://decepticon.red (website)\n  - https://app.decepticon.red (cloud product)\n- **File paths to core implementation**:\n  - Two Docker networks: `decepticon-net` (mgmt: LiteLLM:4000, PostgreSQL:5432, LangGraph:2024, web:3000, Neo4j:7687, BHCE:8081) and `sandbox-net` (Kali, Sliver C2, victim targets). Agent in LangGraph reaches sandbox only via Docker socket\n  - Neo4j is dual-homed (only cross-network service) — sandbox writes findings, agent reads them\n  - 16 specialist agents: Orchestration, Reconnaissance, Exploitation, Post-Exploitation, Vulnerability Research, Domain Specialists (AD, Cloud, Smart Contracts, Reversing, Analyst)\n- **Exact versions**: 4,984 stars, 971 forks, 1 open issue, last commit 2026-08-05, v1.1.40 (2026-07-27), 309 MB repo, created 2025-06-06 (~14 months old).\n- **Raw performance numbers (XBOW validation benchmarks)**:\n  - **102/104 (98.08%) overall**: L1 45/45 (100%), L2 50/51 (98.0%), L3 7/8 (87.5%)\n  - 23 web attack classes covered (XSS 14, Cmd-Inj 8, Default Creds 8, SSTI 7, LFI 6, IDOR 6, etc.)\n  - 6,000+ commits, 50+ docs in `docs/`\n  - Token cost per challenge: 30k-300k tokens, duration 1-45 minutes\n  - Tier-mapped providers: Anthropic, OpenAI, Google Gemini, MiniMax, DeepSeek, xAI, Mistral, OpenRouter, Nvidia NIM, Ollama (local). Subscription OAuth: Claude Max/Pro/Team, ChatGPT Pro/Plus/Team, Gemini Advanced, Copilot Pro, SuperGrok, Perplexity Pro.\n- **Cross-agent leaderboard (from `benchmark-comparison.md`)**:\n  - Decepticon: 98.08% (102/104) — black-box, multi-agent\n  - Shannon Lite (KeygraphHQ): 96.15% (100/104) — white-box, hint-removed\n  - Strix (usestrix): 96.15% (100/104) — black-box\n  - PentestGPT (USENIX '24): 86.5% (90/104) — black-box\n  - Red-MIRROR: 86.0% — black-box, multi-agent + RAG\n  - XBOW (commercial): ~85% — black-box, proprietary\n  - Cyber-AutoAgent: 84.62% (88/104) — black-box\n  - MAPTA: 76.9% (80/104) — black-box, multi-agent\n  - PentestAgent: 50.0% — black-box\n  - AutoPT: 46.0% — black-box\n  - VulnBot: 6.0% — black-box, baseline\n- **Unverified**: Strix's 96.15% uses a slightly different hint level (description only, no vulnerability tags) so the comparison is not strictly apples-to-apples with Decepticon's. **[A]**\n- **Other**: Apache-2.0 license. Per-challenge evidence folders under `benchmark/results/`, with public LangSmith traces. Optional \"Offensive Vaccine\" loop turning findings into defense improvements. Disclaimer: \"Do not use this project on any system or network without explicit written authorization.\" Stack position: red-team agent, not a code auditor; uses code auditors (Semgrep, etc.) as one of its tools.\n\n### nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper\n\n- **Source URLs**:\n  - https://github.com/nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper/main/README.md\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper/contents/skills\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper/main/skills/design/SKILL.md\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper/main/skills/codebase-review/SKILL.md\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper/main/skills/test-audit/SKILL.md\n- **File paths to core implementation**:\n  - 16 skill folders: `caliper-settings, codebase-review, design, design-review, draft-plan, implement, implementation-review, orchestrate, plan-review, pr-create, pr-merge, pr-review, queue, skill-eval, test-audit, usage-guard`\n  - Three sub-packages: `claude-caliper` (all 16), `claude-caliper-workflow` (11), `claude-caliper-tooling` (5: codebase-review + test-audit + skill-eval + queue + usage-guard)\n  - `plan.json` is the single per-task manifest, schema 2; no per-task `.md` files; one `completion.md` per phase\n  - All artifacts under `.claude/claude-caliper/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>/` and gitignored\n- **Exact versions**: 88 stars, 7 forks, 3 open issues, last commit 2026-07-15, v2.0.0 (\"Caliper v2: ceremony proportional to size\"), created 2026-02-25 (~5 months old), 1.4 MB repo.\n- **Raw performance numbers**:\n  - 9-point design-review checklist\n  - TDD enforcement\n  - Fresh-context review (kills self-rationalization bias)\n  - `codebase-review` skill does whole-repo audit with parallel subagents\n  - `test-audit` does suite-level health (A-F grade)\n  - `skill-eval` does assertion-based grading of other skills\n  - Pipeline: design → design-review → implement (TDD) → implementation-review → pr-create → pr-review → pr-merge. Each review is a fresh subagent (no review-by-the-writer)\n  - Large work goes through: draft-plan → plan-review → orchestrate (parallel subagents in worktree isolation)\n- **Unverified**: none material.\n- **Other**: License MIT. \"Caliper\" is a carpentry metaphor (measure twice, cut once) for sizing work, NOT a model benchmark. The only place \"measure\" appears in the model is the `skill-eval` skill (grades *other* skills). Requires Claude Code (not just the API). macOS-only for the queue / usage-guard skills (5-hour window scheduling). Stack position: code workflow layer — design before code, test before merge; `pr-review` is the closest to a traditional code-review agent.\n\n### kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review\n\n- **Source URLs**:\n  - https://github.com/kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review\n  - https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review/main/README.md\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review/commits?per_page=5\n  - https://api.github.com/repos/kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review/contributors\n  - https://aicodereviews.io (website)\n- **File paths to core implementation**:\n  - 7 sections in README: Automated PR Agents, IDE Assistants & Copilots, Key Research & Papers, Security & Static Analysis AI, CLI & Local Workflows, Open Source Models, Benchmarks\n  - Top PR Agents (sampled): CodeRabbit, Kodus, Greptile, Cursor Bugbot, Revieko, Polarity, Conclave AI, AI Change Passport, Ejentum agent-teams, Gito, Ivy Tendril, Optibot, Swarm Orchestrator, heygrc\n  - Top IDE Copilots: GitHub Copilot, Cursor, Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Google Antigravity\n  - Top Security/SAST AI: Snyk DeepCode, Semgrep AI\n  - Top CLI/Local: Aider, Mentat, OpenCommit, prpack, Signum, h5i\n  - Top Benchmarks: Code Review Benchmark, SWE-bench, ReviewBenchLite, ShopPay Audit Benchmark\n- **Exact versions**: 39 stars, 33 forks, 10 open issues, last commit 2026-06-28, created 2025-12-17 (~8 months old), 38 KB repo, 19 contributors.\n- **Raw performance numbers**:\n  - ~50 tool entries, 14+ research papers, 4+ benchmarks\n  - Top contributor (malinosqui, a Kodus engineer) has 27 of 30 total commits; the other 18 contributors have 1 commit each → bus factor ~1\n  - Maintainer bias disclosed in README (\"as maintainers of Kodus, we are biased\")\n- **Unverified**: none material.\n- **Other**: CC0 license. Language = `null` on the GitHub API (Markdown-only). Stack position: discovery layer for the AI code review niche; the list is scoped to **code REVIEW** (vs. general AI coding tools), plus research papers and benchmarks.\n\n---\n\n## Footnotes\n\n¹⁾ **Sub-niche taxonomy (sub-categories)** — the four sub-categories are\ndefined in §c of this doc. The grouping is the author's synthesis; each\nsub-category shares the \"find issues\" vocabulary but the *mechanism* differs\n(rule-based deterministic vs. LLM-orchestrated vs. workflow plugin vs. meta-list).\nSource: synthesis from README + AGENTS.md + OPENGREP.md. **[A]**\n\n²⁾ **opengrep = 2,879 stars, OCaml 5.3.0, LGPL-2.1, 40 language parsers, v1.26.0 (2026-07-24), 5-vendor consortium** — sources:\n- https://api.github.com/repos/opengrep/opengrep (stars, license, last commit, size)\n- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/opengrep/opengrep/main/OPENGREP.md (OCaml 5.3.0, languages not in Semgrep CE/Pro, multicore)\n- https://api.github.com/repos/opengrep/opengrep/contents/languages (40 language folders)\n- https://api.github.com/repos/opengrep/opengrep/releases/latest (v1.26.0)\n- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/opengrep/opengrep/main/README.md L19-22 (consortium: Aikido, Amplify, Endor Labs, Kodem, Orca Security)\n\n³⁾ **Deterministic vs AI-assisted split** — react-doctor's `packages/react-doctor/README.md` says \"Your agent writes bad React, this catches it\" and the mechanism is \"AST scans of `.tsx`/`.jsx` files → 5 categories of checks → JSON diagnostics → health score 0-100 → terminal / JSON / PR comment. No LLM.\" opengrep's `OPENGREP.md` confirms no LLM in the engine. RepoDoctor, ai-code-audit, Decepticon, and claude-caliper all use LLMs as the analysis engine by design (per their respective READMEs). **[F]**\n\n⁴⁾ **Decepticon XBOW 98.08% (102/104) + cross-agent leaderboard** — sources:\n- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/main/benchmark/results/README.md (XBOW L1 45/45 = 100%, L2 50/51 = 98.0%, L3 7/8 = 87.5%, 102/104 = 98.08%)\n- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/main/docs/benchmark-comparison.md (leaderboard with Shannon Lite 96.15%, Strix 96.15%, PentestGPT 86.5%, XBOW commercial ~85%, Cyber-AutoAgent 84.62%, MAPTA 76.9%, PentestAgent 50.0%, AutoPT 46.0%, VulnBot 6.0%)\n\n⁵⁾ **claude-caliper = workflow plugin, not model benchmark** — sources:\n- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper/main/README.md (banner: \"a Claude Code plugin that turns your goal into a PR with as little friction as possible\")\n- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper/main/skills/design/SKILL.md (the \"caliper\" sizes work into Small/Medium/Large; carpentry metaphor \"measure twice, cut once\")\n- The task brief's \"benchmark harness for the model itself\" framing is contradicted by the README. **[A]**\n\n⁶⁾ **Top 3 winners overall** — synthesis combining: opengrep (best general-purpose SAST, fork story), react-doctor (best off-the-shelf React-specific audit, integration breadth), Decepticon (best red-team / attack-side tool, benchmark). See §e best-in-class table for per-dimension winners. **[A]**\n\n⁷⁾ **Roster of 7 repos** — the 7 repos are k1lgor/RepoDoctor, millionco/react-doctor, opengrep/opengrep, kurkowski93/ai-code-audit, PurpleAILAB/Decepticon, nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper, kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review. Sub-category assignments are the author's synthesis from the README + mechanism. **[A]**\n\n⁸⁾ **Methodology (fetch process)** — every repo was fetched via the GitHub REST API (`api.github.com/repos/<owner>/<repo>`) and the raw README. For deeper architecture evidence, the contents API (`api.github.com/repos/<...>/contents/`) was walked for 6 of 7 repos; per-file paths are listed in the Evidence appendix. **[A]**\n\n⁹⁾ **\"Could not verify\" gaps** — claude-caliper mis-description (contradicts task brief), RepoDoctor zero stars (likely hidden), ai-code-audit dormancy (16 mo stale), Decepticon self-reported benchmark (not strictly apples-to-apples with Strix), license ambiguity for ai-code-audit. **[A]**\n\n¹⁰⁾ **RepoDoctor 0 stargazers** — https://api.github.com/repos/k1lgor/RepoDoctor returns `stargazers_count: 0`. Repo was created 2026-02-10 and last pushed 2026-08-03; stars are likely being hidden or the repo was recently transferred/squashed. **[?]**\n\n¹¹⁾ **ai-code-audit dormancy** — 2 stars, 0 forks, 0 issues, last commit 2025-03-12 (over 16 months stale), 18 KB repo. Treated as a \"what LLM-driven audit looks like in LangGraph\" reference architecture, not as a recommended tool. **[A]**\n\n¹²⁾ **Strix vs Decepticon benchmark comparability** — Strix's 96.15% uses a slightly different hint level (description only, no vulnerability tags) so the comparison is not strictly apples-to-apples. Both are black-box; Shannon Lite (96.15%) is white-box. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/main/docs/benchmark-comparison.md. **[A]**\n\n¹³⁾ **License ambiguity** — Decepticon confirmed Apache-2.0 (https://api.github.com/repos/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon); awesome-ai-code-review confirmed CC0 (README bottom badge); ai-code-audit README claims MIT but the GitHub API shows `license: null` (no `LICENSE` file at repo root). **[A]**\n\n¹⁴⁾ **4 sub-categories grouping** — the four sub-categories in this doc are: (1) Repo quality (RepoDoctor, react-doctor), (2) Static analysis / SAST (opengrep), (3) AI-assisted audit (ai-code-audit, Decepticon), (4) Model evaluation + meta-listing (claude-caliper, awesome-ai-code-review). Each sub-category has a distinct mechanism even though the vocabulary (\"find issues,\" \"score a repo\") overlaps. **[A]**\n\n¹⁵⁾ **Repo health scoring (RepoDoctor + react-doctor)** — both produce a 0-100 health score and identify anti-patterns across multiple dimensions. Both are marketed as \"what's wrong with my codebase\" tools. Sources: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/k1lgor/RepoDoctor/main/README.md and https://raw.githubusercontent.com/millionco/react-doctor/main/packages/react-doctor/README.md. **[F]**\n\n¹⁶⁾ **RepoDoctor prompt library** — prompts are first-class artifacts in `src/repodoc/prompts/v1/`. Source: https://api.github.com/repos/k1lgor/RepoDoctor/contents/src/repodoc/prompts. RepoDoctor is \"Copilot-first architecture\" (README \"Why RepoDoctor?\" L60-72). **[F]**\n\n¹⁷⁾ **react-doctor monorepo = 11 packages** — `api`, `core`, `deslop-cli`, `deslop-js`, `eslint-plugin-react-doctor`, `evals`, `fuzz`, `language-server`, `oxlint-plugin-react-doctor`, `react-doctor`, `vscode-react-doctor`, `zed-react-doctor`. Source: https://api.github.com/repos/millionco/react-doctor/contents/packages. **[F]**\n\n¹⁸⁾ **react-doctor \"changed\" scope on PRs** — the GitHub Action's `inputs.scope` defaults to `\"changed\"`, which only reports issues *the PR introduced* vs the base branch, not the existing backlog. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/millionco/react-doctor/main/action.yml L17-23. **[F]**\n\n¹⁹⁾ **opengrep = Semgrep v1.100.0 fork from 2024-12-14** — after Semgrep Inc. moved advanced features (intra-file taint, certain languages) behind a commercial license, the opengrep consortium forked v1.100.0. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/opengrep/opengrep/main/OPENGREP.md. **[F]**\n\n²⁰⁾ **opengrep = 40 language parsers + SARIF output** — sources:\n- https://api.github.com/repos/opengrep/opengrep/contents/languages (40 directories: apex, bash, cairo, circom, cpp, crystal, csharp, dart, dockerfile, elixir, go, hack, html, java, javascript, json, jsonnet, julia, kotlin, lisp, lua, move_on_aptos, move_on_sui, ocaml, php, promql, protobuf, python, ql, r, regexp, ruby, rust, scala, solidity, swift, terraform, typescript, vbnet, yaml)\n- README \"To obtain SARIF output\" section: `--sarif-output=sarif.json` produces SARIF 2.1.0 with metavariables, fingerprints, enclosing context\n\n²¹⁾ **opengrep Trusted Code Base (TCB/)** — `Cap.ml`, `CapUnix.ml`, `CapSys.ml` are capability-based IO sandboxes for the OCaml stdlib, lifted from Semgrep. Source: https://api.github.com/repos/opengrep/opengrep/contents/TCB. **[F]**\n\n²²⁾ **opengrep consortium (5 vendors)** — Aikido, Amplify, Endor Labs, Kodem, Orca Security. Manifesto at https://opengrep.dev/. Open governance: \"contributions accepted on merit, not commercial interest.\" Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/opengrep/opengrep/main/README.md L19-22. **[F]**\n\n²³⁾ **opengrep Cosign-signed releases** — self-contained binaries (~30-44 MB) via Nuitka, signed with Cosign. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/opengrep/opengrep/main/OPENGREP.md L20-27. **[F]**\n\n²⁴⁾ **AI-assisted audit (ai-code-audit + Decepticon)** — both use LLMs as the analysis engine; both run as multi-agent systems; both produce Markdown / Neo4j-persisted reports. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kurkowski93/ai-code-audit/main/README.md and https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/main/README.md. **[F]**\n\n²⁵⁾ **Decepticon version + scale** — v1.1.40 released 2026-07-27, 4,984 stars, 309 MB repo, Apache-2.0, 16 specialist agents. Sources: https://api.github.com/repos/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon and https://api.github.com/repos/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/releases. **[F]**\n\n²⁶⁾ **claude-caliper = 16 skills, design doc gate, fresh subagents** — sources:\n- https://api.github.com/repos/nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper/contents/skills (16 skill folders)\n- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper/main/skills/design/SKILL.md (9-point design-review checklist, Small/Medium/Large sizing)\n\n²⁷⁾ **awesome-ai-code-review = 50+ tools, 14+ papers, 4+ benchmarks** — sources:\n- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review/main/README.md (7 sections: PR Agents, IDE Copilots, Research Papers, Security/SAST AI, CLI/Local, Open-Source Models, Benchmarks)\n\n²⁸⁾ **awesome-ai-code-review = Kodus-maintained, 19 contributors, CC0** — sources:\n- https://api.github.com/repos/kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review/contributors (19 contributors; top contributor malinosqui has 27/30 commits → bus factor ~1)\n- README bottom \"License\" badge: CC0 (Public Domain)\n- README \"Maintainer Note\" discloses Kodus as maintainer (vendor bias acknowledged)\n\n²⁹⁾ **RepoDoctor matrix row sources** — README \"Architecture\" diagram; `src/repodoc/core/copilot.py` (Copilot CLI subprocess wrapper); `pyproject.toml` (Python 3.11+, typer>=0.12.0, pydantic v2). Maturity: 0 stars (hidden), v0.2.0, 2 releases, last commit 2026-08-03, 266 KB repo, \"Development Status :: 3 - Alpha\". Sources: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/k1lgor/RepoDoctor/main/README.md + https://raw.githubusercontent.com/k1lgor/RepoDoctor/main/pyproject.toml + https://api.github.com/repos/k1lgor/RepoDoctor. **[F]**\n\n³⁰⁾ **react-doctor matrix row sources** — packages: `api`, `core`, `deslop-cli`, `deslop-js`, `eslint-plugin-react-doctor`, `evals`, `fuzz`, `language-server`, `oxlint-plugin-react-doctor`, `react-doctor`, `vscode-react-doctor`, `zed-react-doctor`. Mechanism: AST-based, 5 categories. CI: action.yml 14 inputs / 6 outputs, `inputs.scope` default \"changed\", `inputs.blocking` default \"none\". Sources: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/millionco/react-doctor/main/packages/react-doctor/README.md + https://raw.githubusercontent.com/millionco/react-doctor/main/action.yml + https://api.github.com/repos/millionco/react-doctor. **[F]**\n\n³¹⁾ **opengrep matrix row sources** — primary stack OCaml 5.3.0 with shared-memory parallelism; self-contained binaries via Nuitka; mechanism = AST pattern matching + YAML DSL + taint analysis (`--taint-intrafile`); 40 language parsers; SARIF 2.1.0 output. Install: `curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/opengrep/opengrep/main/install.sh | bash` (Linux/macOS), `irm ... install.ps1 | iex` (Windows). License LGPL-2.1. Sources: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/opengrep/opengrep/main/README.md + https://raw.githubusercontent.com/opengrep/opengrep/main/OPENGREP.md + https://api.github.com/repos/opengrep/opengrep. **[F]**\n\n³²⁾ **ai-code-audit matrix row sources** — StateGraph in `my_agent/agent.py`: `START → starting_node → {repository_scanner | answer_question}`; `repository_scanner → 5 perspective nodes in parallel`; all 5 → `generate_report` → `END`; `answer_question → END`. Inputs: GitHub repo URL + `OPENAI_API_KEY` + optional `GITHUB_TOKEN` + `TAVILY_API_KEY`. Distribution: `pip install -r requirements.txt`, then `langgraph run agent`. Sources: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kurkowski93/ai-code-audit/main/my_agent/agent.py + https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kurkowski93/ai-code-audit/main/langgraph.json + https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kurkowski93/ai-code-audit/main/requirements.txt. **[F]**\n\n³³⁾ **Decepticon matrix row sources** — Python (LangGraph) + LiteLLM + Docker Compose + PostgreSQL + Neo4j + tmux. 16 specialist agents: Orchestration, Reconnaissance, Exploitation, Post-Exploitation, Vulnerability Research, Domain Specialists (AD, Cloud, Smart Contracts, Reversing, Analyst). Two-network Docker: `decepticon-net` (mgmt) + `sandbox-net` (ops). Sources: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/main/README.md + https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/main/docs/architecture.md. **[F]**\n\n³⁴⁾ **claude-caliper matrix row sources** — Shell-driven Claude Code plugin, 16 skills, `plan.json` (schema 2). Pipeline: design → design-review → implement (TDD) → implementation-review → pr-create → pr-review → pr-merge. Each review is a fresh subagent. Distribution: `/plugin marketplace add nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper; /plugin install claude-caliper@claude-caliper`. License MIT. Sources: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper/main/README.md + https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper/main/skills/design/SKILL.md. **[F]**\n\n³⁵⁾ **awesome-ai-code-review matrix row sources** — Markdown-only (`language: null`); 7 sections; ~50 tool entries, 14+ research papers, 4+ benchmarks. Distribution: read the Markdown on GitHub or at https://aicodereviews.io. License: CC0 (Public Domain). Sources: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review/main/README.md + https://api.github.com/repos/kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review. **[F]**\n\n³⁶⁾ **Best-in-class per dimension** — the 16-dimension best-in-class table summarizes per-column winners. Each row references a specific README section, line range, or supporting file (e.g., README L46-60 for \"Purpose (clarity)\" Decepticon, OPENGREP.md for \"Primary language / stack\" opengrep). **[A]**\n\n³⁷⁾ **Tally fix (opengrep=7, Decepticon=5, react-doctor=3-4)** — this is the corrected tally from the §e best-in-class table. Opengrep wins dimensions 2, 3, 5, 10, 12, 13, 14 (7 total). Decepticon wins 1, 4, 6, 8, 15 (5 total). react-doctor wins 7, 11, 16 outright (3 total) and shares dimension 9 (Cost model) with opengrep as a tie (so 3-4 counting the tie). The previous \"5 / 4 / 4\" prose summary was incorrect; the table is the source of truth. **[A]**\n\n³⁸⁾ **Category winners** — the 7 category winners in §f are: RepoDoctor/react-doctor for repo health scoring, react-doctor for React-specific audit, opengrep for static analysis, ai-code-audit/claude-caliper for AI-assisted code review, Decepticon for LLM security / red-team, claude-caliper (with caveat) for measuring Claude, awesome-ai-code-review for discovering more tools. **[A]**\n\n³⁹⁾ **RepoDoctor \"Copilot-first architecture\"** — adding a new check is a new prompt template, not a new AST rule. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/k1lgor/RepoDoctor/main/README.md \"Why RepoDoctor?\" L60-72. **[F]**\n\n⁴⁰⁾ **react-doctor = full React-specific specialist** — 14,279 stars, 11 packages, fuzz + evals, \"changed scope\" PR mode, blocking PR mode, 0-100 health score as CI output, oxlint plugin for editor-time feedback, language server for IDE integration. Sources: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/millionco/react-doctor/main/packages/react-doctor/README.md + https://raw.githubusercontent.com/millionco/react-doctor/main/action.yml. **[F]**\n\n⁴¹⁾ **opengrep = Semgrep v1.100.0 fork** — keeps the entire Semgrep rule ecosystem working (OWASP, CWE Top 25, plus thousands of community rules) while moving advanced features (intra-file taint analysis, Apex/Elixir/Crystal/Visual Basic, OCaml 5.3.0 multicore, Cosign-signed binaries) into the open. Backed by a 5-vendor AppSec consortium. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/opengrep/opengrep/main/OPENGREP.md. **[F]**\n\n⁴²⁾ **ai-code-audit = 5-perspective parallel graph** — LangGraph StateGraph in `my_agent/agent.py` decomposes audit by viewpoint (architectural, business/domain, code quality, security, modernization), then aggregates. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kurkowski93/ai-code-audit/main/my_agent/agent.py. **[F]**\n\n⁴³⁾ **Decepticon = 98.08% XBOW + public LangSmith traces** — sources:\n- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/main/benchmark/results/README.md (per-challenge evidence folders, public LangSmith traces)\n- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/main/docs/benchmark-comparison.md (cross-agent leaderboard)\n\n⁴⁴⁾ **claude-caliper does not measure Claude as a model** — the README is explicit that it is a workflow plugin (\"a Claude Code plugin that turns your goal into a PR with as little friction as possible\"). The only \"measurement\" surface is `skill-eval` (grades other skills). Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nikhilsitaram/claude-caliper/main/README.md. **[F]**\n\n⁴⁵⁾ **awesome-ai-code-review = discovery layer** — 50+ tools, 14+ research papers, 4+ benchmarks across 7 categories. Maintained by Kodus. CC0 licensed. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review/main/README.md. **[F]**\n\n⁴⁶⁾ **Cross-cutting findings** — 10 findings covering: deterministic vs AI-assisted axis (1), opengrep/semgrep fork significance (2), 0-100 health score convention (3), model evaluation is its own discipline (4), meta-list proliferation (5), audit-grade tools CI-gated (6), missing compliance/dependency/SBOM/fuzzing/formal-verification/test-quality tools (7), compliance/regulation story underdeveloped (8), \"caliper\" metaphor is workflow not measurement (9), Decepticon's two-network isolation as security template (10). **[A]**\n\n⁴⁷⁾ **opengrep consortium (5 vendors)** — Aikido, Amplify, Endor Labs, Kodem, Orca Security. Forked v1.100.0 after Semgrep Inc. moved advanced features (intra-file taint, certain languages) behind a commercial license. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/opengrep/opengrep/main/README.md L19-22. **[F]**\n\n⁴⁸⁾ **0-100 health score convention** — both RepoDoctor and react-doctor expose a 0-100 health score. RepoDoctor's score is LLM-generated and opaque; react-doctor's is computed from error/warning counts and is reproducible. The \"0-100\" convention is also used in code quality tools like Code Climate (out of roster). Sources: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/k1lgor/RepoDoctor/main/README.md + https://raw.githubusercontent.com/millionco/react-doctor/main/packages/react-doctor/README.md. **[A]**\n\n⁴⁹⁾ **Meta-list overlap** — the \"AI coding\" and \"AI code review\" niches are close enough that the lists overlap (Cursor, Claude Code, Copilot appear in both). The narrower scope of awesome-ai-code-review (review-specific + papers + benchmarks) is what differentiates it from general AI coding tools lists. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kodustech/awesome-ai-code-review/main/README.md. **[A]**\n\n⁵⁰⁾ **Decepticon two-network isolation** — Management on `decepticon-net` (LLM proxy, DBs, API), operations on `sandbox-net` (Kali, C2, targets). The agent reaches the sandbox only via the Docker socket (no TCP from management to operations). Neo4j is the only cross-network service because the sandbox writes findings and the agent reads them. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PurpleAILAB/Decepticon/main/docs/architecture.md. **[F]**\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
